/** siman 451 part 3 — hagalas kelim */
export const fixes = {
  "output/siman_451/biur-halacha/part-001.txt": {
    "8:_": "And one stone vessel — and likewise dung vessels [Chayei Adam]; and see in Shibolei HaLeket who expounds on this:",
    "11:_": "Permitted through hagalah — see Chayei Adam general rule 125 law 5 who wrote this first view in the name of \"some say,\" and most poskim hold that libun is required and it is not clear at all, for Rama himself wrote that hagalah suffices and only l'chatchila the practice is to do libun; and even if we say this was not in his text (for some books have this regarding a mortar in seif 16) — nevertheless one can prove thus from what he wrote that light libun suffices, and it is known that this applies only to something one is stringent to whiten but not to something requiring libun by law; and so is proven in Darkei Moshe that by law hagalah suffices here. It appears the citation brought in Rama misled him — for Tur writes in the name of R' Yoel and responsum of Rashba and Tur in the name of Rashi and Rabbenu Tam; if so, many disagree with the first view. In truth it is a printing error, for this is not mentioned here in Tur nor in Tur Yoreh De'ah siman 121 — the entire matter in the name of Rashi and Rabbenu Tam; only Rabbenu Yoel alone requires libun, and his son Avi HaEzri and likewise the Rosh disagree with them; and in Beit Yosef he brought that Rashba too holds libun is required; nevertheless he concludes that for practical law it appears like the Rosh and Avi HaEzri. He also brought that Mordechai and Hagahot Maimoniyot are lenient; and I found in Shibolei HaLeket in the name of the Geonim that its law is hagalah. [In Beit Yosef it implies he learned from Turat HaBayit also for our matter regarding Pesach; and in Rabbeinu Yerucham I found he wrote in the name of Rashba regarding a pan that only for other prohibitions should one be stringent to require libun, but not for chametz — and this is exactly the Mechaber's words in Yoreh De'ah siman 121 seif 4, intending Rashba's distinction from his broad view.] In any event we have several poskim who hold its law is hagalah [Avi HaEzri, Rosh, Shibolei HaLeket in the name of responsum of the Geonim, Hagahot Maimoniyot, Mordechai; those who forbid are Rabbenu Yoel and Rashba according to Beit Yosef's understanding as above]; and I found that Maharam Chalavah too holds like Rashba. In any event most poskim hold to be lenient, and b'dieved certainly hagalah suffices.",
    "13:_": "Since it was made anew — I do not understand from where: if it is possible to scrape even if absorption preceded — it is not comparable to a patch; and if it is impossible to scrape ever, libun is required [Magen Alef]. In my humble opinion Rama's intent regarding impressions made by lead adhesion on the vessel and not engraved in its body — in that case it is truly permitted in all respects if one can scrape well.",
    "16:_": "That all such is hagalah of kli rishon — Peri Chadash expanded on this that it is not literally kli rishon and this does not help for something used on fire in literal kli rishon; the main advice of placing stones does not help except to raise the boiling upward — but specifically for a vessel whose use is by pouring and not a vessel used in kli rishon. He concludes that perhaps the poskim wrote this only here regarding a mortar, and likewise above in seif 6 regarding large bowls whose main use is by pouring from kli rishon and all such; and Peri Megadim in Eshel Avraham wrote similarly in seif 6 in the name of Bach; and see there what I wrote that one should permit only when it is not ben yomo.",
    "17:א": "Require hagalah — meaning by essential law; but the Mechaber already wrote above in siman 442 that one should not rely on what they clean and wash them in hot water — and this is from the side of the good and proper [Bach above siman 442, and likewise Maamar Mordechai; he added that this is Rama's intent in the gloss]. Magen Avraham wrote there that it deals when the kneading trough is made of many boards and because it is impossible to clean between the cracks; but here Tur and Shulchan Aruch deal when made of one piece. Accordingly, from Rama's conclusion \"and this was already explained above\" it implies it deals in one manner; and therefore Bach's answer is required and from Eshel Avraham. Eshel Avraham agreed there to the law of Magen Avraham's answer that when made of one piece it is permitted to keep after scraping well; nevertheless regarding use on Pesach he is stringent l'chatchila even after hagalah — and according to him Rama's law here applies even when made of one piece. Likewise Magen Avraham who wrote the reason for the custom is because we practice like the view that hagalah does not help for a yeast container — it is obvious that even one piece, although one can scrape well, nevertheless absorption does not exit through hagalah.",
    "17:ב": "And the custom not to use — see Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot who is uncertain perhaps one may permit through peeling with artisan tools and hagalah like a mortar above; at first glance I wondered — behold in Chok Yaakov he brings in the name of Terumat HaDeshen and Mahari Weil to be stringent even through peeling; but afterward I saw Terumat HaDeshen and Mahari Weil did not say this except regarding something whose use is through fire such as a shovel; and Levush implies he is stringent even through hagalah in all cases. In my humble opinion it appears one should decide: if it is one piece and has no cracks, one may permit through peeling and hagalah; if made of several pieces, one may not permit even through peeling.",
    "17:ג": "On Pesach — see Mishna Berurah the reason because it is impossible to clean; and so several Acharonim; and from this metal vessels that can be cleaned are permitted through hagalah — and so truly Chayei Adam and Peri Chadash and Mekor Chayyim. However, it requires study — they should have been concerned for those who are stringent above seif 16 that Rama wrote there that the practice is l'chatchila to whiten; and perhaps a metal kneading trough that is wide and thin is hard to whiten because it is apt to break — therefore we are lenient with hagalah, similar to Rama's leniency there for wooden vessels because libun is impossible; but where it will not break, it is proper and good l'chatchila to be stringent; and so appears in Taz s.k. 27; and see Peri Megadim who also wrote that according to Magen Avraham one should be stringent even for metal.",
    "22:א": "House of yeast and charoset — see responsum of Chavot Yair siman 120 who discussed kashering yeast containers in which they burn yayin nesech; fortunate is one who can make new ones, but they would not heed to forbid entirely; I commanded before hagalah to cook in the container and reeds and the hat to cook in them water with ash called lye, also much ash so they are very strong — and they impair the taste; and great supervision is needed to clean the container at its bottom and remove the reeds through an artisan, or place coals on the place of the patch; and he concluded there that after all this one who guards his soul should distance from drinking yayin nesech on Pesach — but one should not forbid them after these repairs; and see Chiddushei R' Akiva Eiger on \"and it appears\" etc.",
    "22:ב": "But for pottery libun does not help — see Biur HaGra who wrote this is only according to Rav Hai Gaon brought in Tur that his view is hagalah does not help for pottery even if used only cold; but according to what he ruled in seif 21 that hagalah helps, the same applies here; see there. It requires study regarding other poskim — for from their view it appears that although there we hold hagalah helps, here it is worse.",
    "22:ג": "Winter-house oven — this is what is made of kakalin to heat the winter house. See Mishna Berurah that the prohibition here is because heating fired from inside does not help to expel what was absorbed from its back — so too Magen Avraham and Gra; but regarding the face itself it simply appears heating helps like other ovens. Some Acharonim entirely rejected heating a winter-house oven to kasher it (see Eshel Avraham and Chayei Adam). Chayei Adam wrote: it appears what the Acharonim were stringent about regarding a winter-house oven — that libun does not help — deals like what they do in Poland where the oven is made only of kakalin and it is literally a pottery vessel; but in this country it is known they build around the kakalin a partition of bricks and its law is literally like an oven in which one bakes — regarding this we are not concerned lest they crack; therefore it appears all agree libun or coating with clay helps; and the same for ovens called in our country \"oven\" or \"ofen\" that have a cover — libun helps; therefore it appears in a place where one has no stove to cook, one may permit whitening winter-house ovens in Lithuania and the like where ovens are made thus. In essential law, even one made only of kakalin, the Acharonim agreed libun helps — since they heat from inside, the concern lest they crack does not apply; and Levush too wrote on this that it is extra stringency. In any event in our ovens certainly one should not be stringent as Chayei Adam wrote. What Chayei Adam wrote about ovens in our country etc. that have a cover — I do not know why he requires specifically a cover, for behold regarding kuvia in seif 2, if filled with coals from inside it helps even without a cover; and likewise regarding pots in Gemara it was permitted with filling coals only because of concern lest they crack though there is no cover — if so, there is no distinction here.",
    "23:_": "Coated with glass — their law is like pottery vessels. See Mishna Berurah what he wrote that it is not comparable to seif 26 because it is coated on the pottery and fused with it in the kiln — its law is like pottery; so too the Rosh. However in Tosafot Avodah Zarah daf 33 they wrote to distinguish between whole glass vessels that do not absorb where it is only a coating on pottery — they brought thus from Arukh; accordingly it is not specifically because it was fused in the kiln. Gra in seif 26 brought only Tosafot's view, and so from his words in s.k. 86 it appears he explained Rama's words there regarding silver vessels with glass lining — it applies even per the Mechaber there who permits glass, and that case is different because it is only lining in general, similar to a handle; meaning there is no distinction between pottery and glass."
  },
  "output/siman_451/ateret-zekenim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Pottery pots, etc. One may not place anything on a winter-house oven because it is a pottery vessel that never leaves its absorbed state; and likewise one may not heat with kakalin during the days of Pesach for the same reason (from R' Isserles in derashot); and b'dieved some forbid even if one returns and coats fresh clay on Pesach to the thickness of a finger (my teacher in Bach in the name of my elder relative the gaon Maharar Hirsch Shor); and some permit b'dieved (Maharam of Lublin in his responsum siman 106); but pottery vessels lined with molten lead are permitted through hagalah like other metal vessels (Beit Yosef and Mordechai and Maharam Isserles); and a metal pot — if one wishes to fill it with coals from inside — is permitted (Tur and Maharam Isserles):",
    "3:_": "Knives — if the sheath has crevices, or if it has nails in the sheath, hagalah does not help for them; and such is the practice and one should not change. Behold, there are three levels regarding a knife: (1) by law — hagalah in boiling water; above that — to whiten the iron in fire; and the sheath in boiling water if it is not of wood or deer horn; above that — entirely new; and the author mentioned only the higher level. One must remove all rust — that is, what we call rost — and some say that rust itself does not help with hagalah; but sometimes when there are black or red spots in some place and there is no substance there, only appearance alone, one may kasher it after scraping all that is possible; nevertheless it is better to whiten that place.",
    "6:_": "And large bowls, etc. And specifically when he cannot insert them into a kli rishon; but one may not do so at all with a bowl or any item that can be inserted into a kli rishon (Maharam Isserles). And large bowls — one pours boiling water specifically from the vessel so that it becomes kli rishon, and one should not draw with another vessel from a vessel containing boiling water unless one left the vessel used for drawing inside the kli rishon (Maharil). And it appears per our custom that we pour boiling water onto the stones — then the water returns to boiling and then even hot water suffices (Maharikh likewise). And one should not kasher a drinking vessel together with other vessels lest they absorb from the hagalah water; and likewise when kashering before six (Maharam Isserles).",
    "13:_": "A vessel that has a patch — it appears that wherever there is a patch and the adhesion is from fire such that chametz cannot enter there b'eyna, then hagalah before the adhesion helps; but if there is not strong adhesion, what does hagalah before adhesion help — for there is concern lest chametz enter there b'eyna at the time of adhesion; therefore it does not help unless one places coals there to burn the chametz. All this is regarding a patch that is attached with nails; but a patch that artisans make on a tin or silver vessel — there is no concern here, for through the heat what was absorbed in it is burned, as above; and it is simple. One should pound all spices before Pesach, and if he did not do so it is permitted on Pesach itself if he kashered it before Pesach. The practice is not to kasher a vessel in which yayin nesech was — as Levush writes — and it appears that if one cooked in it with water and earth until the smell entirely departed, there is no prohibition to kasher it afterward.",
    "22:_": "And the winter-house oven — some permit b'dieved (She'eilot uTeshuvot R' Moshe of Lublin siman 106); some are stringent even if one returns and coats fresh clay the thickness of a finger. It appears that if one does not use matzah there but only a Pesach pot, it is obvious there is no prohibition b'dieved even in a place where all year they are accustomed to place hot chametz pots there — not as my teacher wrote in Beit Chadash in the name of Maharash Hirsch in siman 447 note 1. It appears there is no prohibition at all in any manner b'dieved even for eating, unless he left matzah there as it is without a pot while it is boiling — then one must forbid b'dieved in a place where they are accustomed each year to place chametz there, as above; it is simple as written in siman 447 seif 1 that there is no extra stringency in a chametz mixture over other prohibited mixtures except regarding mashahu — there is no prohibition in two pots, one forbidden and one permitted, that touched each other, as Rama wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 92 seif 8 and see there; and in responsum of R' Moshe of Lublin. Beer barrels of wood — hagalah does not help to kasher at all; and so ruled Maharshal and Maharash of Lublin and Mohar Hirsch Shor (my teacher in Beit Chadash). Likewise a barrel of burnt wine made from grain — it is obvious hagalah does not help (Maharam Isserles); but honey-water barrels may be permitted through hagalah.",
    "23:_": "A pottery vessel lined inside with tin — even though they did not libun it until sparks fly, only until the lead melts and covers them from inside — nevertheless it is considered better than hagalah (responsum of Maharam siman 403; he ruled to permit for three reasons):"
  },
  "output/siman_451/chatam-sofer/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "In Magen Avraham s.k. 1 — one may kasher them. In my humble opinion: although in Yoreh De'ah he ruled knives require libun because of minority use through fire — one may say chametz is a minority of a minority through fire; and it is simple.",
    "2:_": "(There in Mechaber seif 4 in the gloss) that they are stringent to whiten — in my humble opinion one need not be concerned that through the heat of light libun the sauce absorbed in the thickness of the vessel wall will be re-absorbed through this fire and require complete libun; that is, the sauce in the thickness of the vessel does not see the face of the fire and a thin partition separates between the fire and the absorbed sauce — and it is regarded as cooking and does not require libun.",
    "3:_": "(There in Mechaber seif 6) the citation s.k. 17 of Magen Avraham was deleted and should read next to the word \"sometimes\" below in Rama.",
    "4:_": "Except with fat. In my humble opinion see Shach siman 121 s.k. 8 and 88 and Pri To'ar — and none understood the words of Rama in siman 96 as he wrote there; and most things gentiles fry are fat and milk which is itself a prohibition — therefore libun is required. The same applies to our milk pan in which we fried meat — since the absorbing fat is also a prohibition together with milk, a milk pan requires libun; not so a sour pan where chametz was absorbed through fat that is not chametz and is a permissible medium — hagalah helps. These are his words of blessed memory; and from this it is understood that a pan in which we fried with permitted fat — hagalah helps if one wants to kasher it to cook milk in it, it does not require libun because the permitted was absorbed, as above s.k. 11; except we do not practice kashering a meat vessel for milk or vice versa, as below in Magen Avraham siman 509 s.k. 11. But essentially whenever permitted was absorbed, hagalah suffices — as regarding a spit of a chatas at the end of Avodah Zarah; except chametz is called prohibited absorption — therefore his reasoning above is required; and see Chavot Yair Yoreh De'ah siman 111.",
    "5:_": "(There in Magen Avraham s.k. 27) prohibition b'eyna — in my humble opinion: and burn a little to ash before it absorbs through coals.",
    "6:_": "(There in Magen Avraham note 37 regarding) \"it is written in the gloss, etc., that it was already repaired in the first matzah\" and if they became mixed, etc. — in my humble opinion it should read: and in any case if they became mixed, etc.; and he means with matzot that remained in a new mortar:",
    "7:_": "And it implies in Terumat HaDeshen there regarding yayin nesech barrels — in my humble opinion: and kashering yeast containers of yayin nesech; see responsa Chavot Yair Orach Chayim siman 120.",
    "8:_": "Kakalin and becomes like a small oven — in my humble opinion: called \"ofen\"; and see below siman 461 s.k. 2. But kuvia etc.; and see Chavot Yair Orach Chayim siman 130."
  },
  "output/siman_451/dagul-merevavah/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "It is not mentioned there anything of this; however this law is in siman 105 seif 7 in the gloss, and it implies there in Shach that regarding prohibition of oil absorbed in a vessel it is possible the entire vessel becomes forbidden; see there Shach s.k. 23."
  },
  "output/siman_451/eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "[1] And one hides them, etc. — Beit Chadash quarreled with those who place iron chametz vessels at height for decoration, and likewise Pesach vessels or Kutim vessels pawned the rest of the year; see there. And in Eliyahu Zuta I upheld the custom to be lenient, and all the Acharonim agreed; and it is not comparable to a candle two stories high, for there it is a d'oraisa prohibition — which is not so here, for stam vessels are not ben yomo; furthermore regarding eating we are more careful, as Tosafot Chullin wrote regarding the incident of R' Pinchas ben Yair; they also place markers on vessels permitted on Pesach so they will not err.",
    "2:_": "[2] [Levush] except in a vessel, etc. — the second wanted to say: even if one did not use it even via pouring, only in kli sheini — nevertheless heating does not help; and in such a manner he wrote in seif 5 regarding hagalah; and even though in siman 447 he wrote it is good to be stringent with kli sheini — here l'chatchila it is reasonable to him that by law they are forbidden.",
    "3:_": "[3] If one returns them to the kiln, etc. — and we practice not to fire them (Issur veHeter siman 46).",
    "4:_": "[4] Forbidden to use, etc. — in Eliyahu Zuta I explained: specifically when fixed permanently, for we are concerned perhaps he will use it hot; and there is no distinction between in its usual manner in rinsing — meaning Rama concluded; see Yoreh De'ah siman 121 — he wanted to say that there it is explained for this even regarding other prohibitions; and so it appears in Shach siman 91 end note 2, and not like Olat Shabbat. Also what he understood from what Levush wrote on Pesach — it comes to exclude erev Pesach after six hours when it is permitted — is not so, for after six hours is also called Pesach and forbidden for the reason mentioned above.",
    "5:_": "[5] And if one fills them, etc. — this is the language of Beit Yosef from Rashba in Turat HaBayit; it appears he did not have in the Gemara \"and if one fills coals it is fine\" — for he wrote: if in a vessel whose way is not to heat from inside like a pot, even if they heated from inside it is forbidden lest one be concerned; and if one returns it to the kiln it is permitted — until here. And the same law applies to kuvia whose way is not to heat from inside; and possibly Rashba concedes regarding kuvia since it is made of bricks and clay — until here Beit Yosef's language. And in Chok Yaakov he elaborated to wonder: what did Beit Yosef wonder on Rashba — behold this distinction of kuvia is proven at the end of the siman where he says if one fills coals it is permitted; see there. And it is not difficult at all — for in any event since Rashba did not bring there the law of filling coals, it entered his mind he did not have that girsa; and furthermore in the Gemara one may say that even in pots it is permitted with filling coals, as he wrote; and in his honor he did not see except in Turat HaBayit page 46, but it escaped him on page 33 where he ruled explicitly that filling coals is permitted; also in the long Turat HaBayit of Rashba page 83 he did not see, where he copied the Gemara \"and if one fills coals it is fine\" and has the girsa like our Gemara; also one may wonder on him — for it is explicit to Rashba there that he permits even in a pottery kuvia with filling coals; and so it appears in Shulchan Aruch and Levush who wrote \"and likewise a small oven called pidila,\" etc., and it is a pottery vessel as Beit Yosef wrote at the beginning of siman 461; and so I proved in my Yoreh De'ah at the beginning of siman 97 from Tosafot Pesachim page 30 — for if not, what do they challenge on that kuvia that it is permitted with filling coals — behold the Torah testified regarding pottery vessels that they never leave their mouths; see there — behold kuvia deals with bricks and clay; but perforce it is reasonable to him it deals with pottery; and so Rambam explained in chapter 5 of Laws of Chametz. Also I wondered on Bach here who wrote in the name of Tosafot to the contrary that it deals with bricks and clay. And that which is established for us that heating from outside does not help — one may say: specifically pots, for we are concerned; which is not so in kuvia, for we are not concerned even if of pottery; and so is explained in Rokeach end siman 484; and all this I wrote briefly in Eliyahu Zuta. Furthermore one may say: filling coals that the Gemara permits refers also to pots — for only regarding mere heating we say we are concerned, which is not so with filling coals, for we see we are not concerned; and it is comparable to returning to the kiln; and so appears the view of Magen Avraham; and so it appears in Tosafot there; and so is explicit in Piskei Tosafot chapter Kol Sha'ah siman 88; and all this is not like Shibolei Leket and the baraita who explained that filling coals permitted deals specifically with an oven they heated from inside.",
    "6:_": "[6] One kashered, etc. — since permitted was absorbed, but prohibited absorption — I explained in Yoreh De'ah end siman 121 that libun is required; and Mateh Moshe wrote that also on Pesach one who is stringent to whiten — a blessing will come upon him; and so it appears in Magen Avraham, because sometimes they stick bread in a knife called pfeinitz near the fire, or stick pashitida or pankoken near the fire; and in practice, if possible with new ones it is praiseworthy. And where the sheath is of wood, Mahari Weil siman 153 wrote that by law hagalah does not help — we are concerned for it; and likewise of wood, since it sticks with adhesion we are concerned; and Bach wrote the same law if the sheath is fixed with nails — presumably there are crevices in them; and per this we do not find the law of hagalah in knives unless the sheath too is of the knife's kind, such as entirely metal; and it requires study — behold on Pesachim page 30 it says regarding their sheath of clay and iron in fire, and the halachah is both in boiling water; and Rashi explained their sheath of clay to protect them lest the fire burn them — until here; it implies it deals with a wooden sheath, and even so hagalah in boiling water helps.",
    "7:_": "[7] While it is still boiling, etc. — meaning yad soledes bo; see siman 318 seif 9. And it is not correct to place a white-hot stone on the knife and pour boiling water on them as written in seif 6 — because in a stone vessel placed inside it works well, which is not so with a knife (Magen Avraham); and per what I wrote in Bach's name there that a white-hot stone is not like kli rishon — in any event it is forbidden.",
    "8:_": "(8) On a whetstone, etc. — and the same law applies to any other item that removes rust — hagalah suffices (Bach):",
    "9:_": "[9] All rust, etc. — Taz explained: specifically what we call rost when there is substance; and when one scrapes from there there is like dust; but when there is blackness or redness and it is impossible to remove the substance entirely even after scraping — there is no substance, only appearance alone — one kashered it after scraping all that is possible; nevertheless in any event one should whiten that place — until here his words briefly. And it requires study — behold he wrote afterward in libun one need not remove rust; if so, why do I need scraping; and possibly specifically when whitening the entire vessel, which is not so when whitening one place. But it is difficult — behold even in actual rust libun helps (instead of libun) in one place, as written in seif 5; and what difference is there between no substance; and one may say: on the side of what is better Taz spoke of libun and not by law; but it requires study — behold Darkei Moshe Yoreh De'ah siman 121 wrote thus: and if one cannot remove the rust, Issur veHeter wrote that even if only a little remains it has no remedy through hagalah — until here; and so Maharil wrote; and possibly it deals with rust that has substance; and in my Yoreh De'ah there I challenged Bach there and Perishah in the name of R' Shlomo in the gloss that it is permitted — and they wondered at their reasoning that it is fit to forbid; and they did not mention the poskim mentioned above who forbade explicitly; however in Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 14 of Laws of Forbidden Foods he wrote to permit — and to this R' Shlomo leaned; and so it appears to me — for it deals with the matter Taz deals with where there is no substance in the rust; and with this everything is resolved b'seder.",
    "10:_": "[10] And libun is required, etc. — in Yoreh De'ah in the gloss there it is explained that b'dieved if they kashered or scraped well and used it hot it does not forbid (Chok Yaakov); and it is difficult — behold it is explicit there specifically without a crevice; but here where there is a crevice hagalah does not help even b'dieved.",
    "11:_": "(11) [Levush] That it is extra stringency, etc. And in Matteh Moshe he wrote the reason that chametz is deep, deep — who can find it; and Magen Avraham wrote that since grease adheres to it b'eyna, if one opens the stitching and scrapes well it may be permitted:",
    "12:_": "(12) Through fire, etc. — meaning without water, such as a spit and poker on which they roast chametz dough dry — see seif 27:",
    "13:_": "[13] And there are those who are lenient, etc. — because permitted was absorbed; and see siman 447 seif 6 — Rashba holds it is called prohibited absorption; if so, even b'dieved one should forbid unless one whitened it; so Magen Avraham wrote; and it appears one may rely in great loss on poskim who hold it is called permitted absorption; and furthermore in Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 17 of Laws of Forbidden Foods he wrote that light libun suffices on Rambam who deals with prohibited absorption; and even though in Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 1 of Laws of Yom Tov he wrote: specifically regarding permitted absorption — meaning there he wrote in the name of Semak and here in the name of R' Avigdor; and furthermore in Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 5 of Laws of Chametz he wrote that regarding chametz, even though its use is literally through fire, hagalah suffices; and so Kolbo siman 48 and Rabbeinu Yerucham page 40 in the name of Rashba wrote; and from this one may wonder on what Magen Avraham wrote in the name of Rashba to the contrary; and see note 38; and even though we do not hold thus — in any event here one may certainly rely.",
    "14:_": "(14) In kli rishon, etc. — see Taz in Yoreh De'ah end siman 94. Kenesset HaGedolah wrote: a prohibited vessel lined with tin — one should be stringent to kasher it, for it is not considered a new vessel; and they immerse it without a blessing:",
    "15:_": "(15) Inside the vessel, etc. — but rust on the outside or on its lip above does not impede (Maharil):",
    "16:_": "[16] [Levush] three times, etc. — it requires study: whence do we know that from all the poskim it appears hagalah once suffices. Maharil wrote: those kashered via pouring should pour quickly and not stand far away from concern of soiling his garments so the stream will not stop — until here; and also he should not take in another vessel from an under-vessel and pour on the kashered item unless it was there when he raised the boiling.",
    "17:_": "(17) Sometimes, etc. — and if it is known that it was used within twenty-four hours in kli rishon, certainly the law of kli rishon applies to it:",
    "18:_": "(18) And some are stringent, etc. — however b'dieved one may rely on the first reasoning:",
    "19:_": "[19] And large bowls, etc. — Bach's view: specifically bowls known not to have been used except via pouring; but if there is concern they were sometimes used also in kli rishon — a white-hot stone does not help, for that is only via pouring — until here. However in Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 5 it appears somewhat that it is like kli rishon; and so is explained in Mahari Weil whom I will bring in note 39; and one may be lenient with all bowls — not with something whose majority use is in kli rishon; and so is explained in Maharil.",
    "20:_": "(20) One places a stone, etc. — and likewise dung vessels (Tur); brick dried in the sun — its law is like an earthenware vessel; but those burned in a kiln — their law is like pottery vessels. Magen Avraham small seif 4 — and heating helps them even in our ovens (Chok Yaakov):",
    "21:_": "(21) All similar items, etc. — such as iron plates on which they bake meat or cheese, for sometimes they place hot water on them — see note 39:",
    "22:_": "(22) And one passes the stone, etc. — and even though in some places the stone did not touch, the boiling water touches there and it is permitted (Taz); but Magen Avraham wrote: bowls that have rims and protrusions like buttons and flowers — it is impossible to pass the stone over all of them — they have no remedy except to insert them into a pot — until here; and perhaps this deals when the water too will not pass there:",
    "23:_": "(23) From horn, etc. — Matteh Yom Tov wrote those made from sheet metal — it appears to me that since it is strong and very hard it is not comparable to horn that softens from heat:",
    "24:_": "[24] And it is good, etc. — Bach and Taz ruled to buy new ones; and the meat itself salted in it — its law is like dry meat, as explained in siman 447; and Magen Avraham's view: even if they salted on them without hagalah on Pesach it is permitted. Our candlesticks — since their way is not to use them with chametz — we do not practice kashering them at all; and Chok Yaakov agreed.",
    "25:_": "[25] Pan, etc. — in seif 16 he wrote the practice is to whiten it; and Magen Avraham wrote: pans in which they bake cakes b'eyna require libun; and in my Yoreh De'ah siman 121 I explained the laws of this siman and siman 452 — therefore I abbreviated here.",
    "26:_": "(26) However b'dieved, etc. — and specifically when it is known they did not use the handles (Sheyarei Kenesset HaGedolah); and Taz forbids knives even b'dieved — it seems from the reason that since it is used all year, presumably one also touches them in the sheath while roasting — and it is as though known; so it appears to me. And if it is a metal vessel and one inserts the handles into the food — I raised this in my book on Yoreh De'ah beginning siman 94: if before they kashered at all it is forbidden, for we say its absorption walks through the whole thing; but after kashering, even though they did not kasher the handles, it is permitted — for we rely on the poskim that as it absorbs so it discharges:",
    "27:_": "(27) One should not forbid, etc. — meaning: if the food was already used it is permitted; but one may not use it again even within Pesach if it cannot be kashered:"
  },
  "output/siman_451/eshel-avraham/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א": "Recognizable. And I would say: for chametz that became mixed — chametz is visible to the eye but not recognizable. See Raavan and Ran and R' Isserles:",
    "1:ב": "Without kashering. A vessel that requires libun — if one kashered it and used it boiling, the dish is forbidden (Rashba and Orchot Chayim); cold is permitted even l'chatchila; see Yoreh De'ah siman 91.",
    "2:_": "Permitted; and Chayei Adam wrote in the name of Shibolei HaLeket that heating from inside does not help for it; and he concludes there that perhaps those made of bricks and dirt help according to everyone — see siman 461 seif 2:",
    "3:_": "And they are permitted: see in Eshel Avraham note 10 — because regarding a knife where there are still sides to be lenient we rely even though one holds chametz is called permitted absorption; and one may say regarding spits b'seder Chayei Adam; and there are those who practice placing a white-hot stone on a knife and pouring boiling water on it — and it is a complete prohibition per the Acharonim.",
    "4:א": "Straw burns. Since chametz is on it, it is called prohibited absorption and hagalah does not suffice. And Taz challenged from the implication of Shulchan Aruch 447 seif 7 and 452 that it is called permitted absorption; see there. And furthermore what he wrote from a pan in seif 11 — it should read that here it deals where they roasted chametz b'eyna in it, which is like an oven in which they baked. And Chayei Adam struck with an axe that one cannot decide in this matter — for sometimes they ruled it as permitted absorption and sometimes etc.; see there.",
    "4:ב": "Libun is required. And he challenged Yoreh De'ah siman 18 — behold we frequently use a trivet for meat and milk vessels. And one may answer: the concern is perhaps porridge spilled from the pot onto it b'eyna; from now on all year there is no concern — behold it is only peel-thickness and the pot remains in place; see there.",
    "5:א": "In kli sheini. And Bach is stringent with them; see Peri Chadash.",
    "5:ב": "Libun. And there are sages who ruled: when they coated it anew with tin one need not kasher it; and R' Shlomo challenged them with three challenges; and Peri Chadash elaborated for his sake and concluded in practice one may not rely on them — for sometimes due to speed the coating does not rise on the entire vessel; and he wrote that such is the practice in Jerusalem. And so Chayei Adam — the great ones of the land are stringent on themselves; and he wrote they should immerse after coating without a blessing.",
    "6:_": "Chayei Adam wrote: specifically for general kashering of vessels — not so chametz, for one may retain them — it is not called b'dieved; and it requires study.",
    "8:_": "In hagalah — Mahari Weil wrote that a copper vessel does not absorb via pouring and does not require hagalah; and Shach Yoreh De'ah siman 98 rejected his words; see there.",
    "10:א": "Hagalah. And that meat is permitted in siman 447 seif 5 — because it was nullified in sixty — this is not in a vessel; see Yoreh De'ah siman 105.",
    "10:ב": "And they appear. And in a place where it is common to find grain kernels in the salt, one should be stringent (Peri Chadash):",
    "12:_": "Their handles. The reason is not because only part of it was heated, etc. — see Yoreh De'ah siman 121; rather lest the house-handle received chametz taste and hot matzah touched it. And therefore one need not distinguish between metal vessels and other vessels — Taz:",
    "13:_": "Coals. See Baer Heitev note 434 what he wrote in the name of Maharam Chalavah Chayei Adam siman 43. And see in Maggid MaReishit siman 1 who wrote they did not agree with him all the sages of his generation; and so Maharal agreed in Eretz Yisrael that hagalah is required; and in Mahari Metrani. See Peri Chadash and Kenesset HaGedolah.",
    "15:_": "Libun — certainly touched on the last boiling:",
    "16:א": "In hagalah. And in siman 446 seif 7 regarding unripe grapes — it is different, for it is nullified one in a hundred; and here on Pesach its prohibition is in any amount.",
    "16:ב": "Copper waste. Bach warns regarding those made from copper waste — one should not whiten with filling coals, lest they are alive on them and crack.",
    "16:ג": "To whiten. And in Peri Chadash he wondered whence this — behold the implication is truly the law applies only to a mortar and bowls whose use is only in kli sheini; see there.",
    "17:_": "Required. And if Levush forbade rubbing — Tur in Yoreh De'ah siman 210 permits it; see there two settled reasons:",
    "19:_": "The bellows. See Baer Heitev; and see responsum of Chayei Adam siman 58.",
    "22:_": "And the charoset house — see Yoreh De'ah siman 69 that in any event chametz was not used in it. And even if we say since its vinegar is sharp it absorbs even when cold — nevertheless one may say not to use them hot. One may rely and permit without rinsing first — go and learn from now: certainly if chametz hot was used in it, rinsing is required to use cold matzah in it. And yayin nesech vessels require rinsing because they are very sharp. If so, due to the stringency of yayin nesech — see responsum Bach Beit Moed siman 18.",
    "23:_": "Glazier. Thus two Mordechais forbid using old porcelain vessels because they tested them in fire and sparks emerged; also after putting water in them it became lighter — behold it absorbed. And he wrote that now they counterfeit it and it is not of the type that enters the category of glass vessels (Peri Chadash):",
    "27:א": "That was not checked. L'chatchila libun is required; and b'dieved we rely on one view that it suffices — for the taste does not return and revive, it is already nullified; and even though in a vessel we do not nullify a prohibition — which is not so — for it is merely a safek.",
    "27:ב": "To rinse them. Hands are busy — and perhaps one touches them and there is chametz on one's hand:"
  },
  "output/siman_451/kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "(1) [Seif 1] Pottery pots, etc. — not specifically pots; likewise bowls whose use is via pouring and kli sheini vessels — the law is the same. Chayei Adam note 1, Kaf HaChayyim note 1 — meaning: even though their use is not through fire like pots, one must also scrub and hide them.",
    "2:_": "(2) There. Pottery pots, etc. — and the same applies to other vessels that are not pottery: if one does not wish to kasher or libun them, one may also use this remedy of scrubbing and preserving them until after Pesach; but he spoke in the present tense regarding other vessels — since they have a remedy, one does not do so but rather kasher or libuns them. Chayei Adam note 4.",
    "3:_": "(3) There — that they were used with chametz, etc. But new vessels bought from the market — even pottery vessels — one need not be concerned lest perhaps they were used, for there is no general way to use new vessels. But metal vessels bought from a gentile in his home, and one does not know them — that they are certainly new — require concern to kasher or whiten them according to their use. Orach Chayyim general rule 58 siman 46. Chayei Adam note 2, Kaf HaChayyim note 2 — and after hagalah or libun one must immerse them, even though they are old, as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 121 seif 1; see there. And furthermore see there siman 122 seif 11. Nevertheless with clear glass vessels one may permit buying from a gentile from his home even if the gentile does not speak offhand and takes the vessel from among the vessels — for since they are clear, certainly they were not yet used. R' Zalman note 5.",
    "4:_": "(4) There — that they were used with chametz, etc. And he wrote in responsum Chakham Tzvi siman 75 a practical halachic ruling to permit a dish cooked on Pesach in a clean chametz pot over which twelve months passed — even for us who forbid nitelaf (as written above siman 447 seif 10) — nevertheless after twelve months there is no taste at all; and nevertheless his heart was not filled to permit cooking in it l'chatchila; and the Gaon R' Naftali Katz z\"l agreed with him there in siman 76; see there. And Beit Din above siman 447 note 10 — also we wrote there the reasoning of Shibolei Leket who permits after two months; see there. However in responsum Panim Me'irot part 1 siman 24 he disagreed; see there. And so Radbaz siman 739 — they did not permit retaining twelve months except regarding wine alone, not other prohibitions; see there. Eshel Avraham note 6 — and so in responsum Chaim Sha'al part 2 siman 38 — he wrote the words of Rashba in responsum siman 575 are not like Chakham Tzvi who wrote there that aging helps only for wine vessels; and he wrote an incident came to his hand in Israel that he was sent a gift of nice porcelain vessels and it was not discernible whether the gentile sender had already used them and two years passed — and they are expensive vessels; and he wrote that at first glance one should not permit l'chatchila as Chakham Tzvi himself wrote; and also from Rashba's words it appears even b'dieved it is forbidden; but here where there is a safek-safek perhaps they were not used — and if we say they were used, safek to be lenient — and still requires answer; until here. And it appears to me that if it is possible to kasher them in boiling water three times one should do so; and if they are vessels there is no way to use boiling — yad soledes bo — one may also be lenient. Shaarei Teshuvah note 1; and see Yoreh De'ah siman 113 seif 16 and siman 121 seif 1 and siman 122 seif 11 — these laws; examine.",
    "5:_": "(5) There. All year. And the same if one used chametz in them within Pesach — the law is thus. Chayei Adam note 3; Levush. R' Zalman siman 447 note 3 — and he means one must scrub them well and hide them, and it is permitted to use them after Pesach because chametz absorbed in a vessel has no prohibition on account of Beit Yosef and Beit Yosef as written below note 8; and so too R' Zalman there.",
    "6:_": "(6) There — even those in which they make porridge, etc. — meaning then they absorbed much chametz; and if one cooks in them after Pesach there will not be sixty — nevertheless it is permitted; and the reason: we are lenient like Rashi who holds chametz after Pesach is not forbidden except due to a mere penalty, and he is penalized only when the chametz exists b'eyna; but what was absorbed in the vessel he is not penalized — therefore it is permitted even l'chatchila to cook in them. Orach Chayyim note 1, Magen Avraham note 1.",
    "7:_": "(7) There — scrub them well, etc. And if one did not scrub — if one scrubs during Pesach or after Pesach, it is not as if he nullified, as explained above siman 435; see there. Chayei Adam note 4, Kaf HaChayyim note 4, R' Zalman note 3 — and that one scrubs during Pesach — this is outside Israel; but on Shabbat and Yom Tov it is forbidden to touch chametz even to destroy it, as written in siman 446; see there. R' Zalman there.",
    "8:_": "(8) There. And it is permitted to retain them, etc. — and it is not comparable to chametz that became mixed, which is forbidden to retain — for there it appears but is not recognizable; but absorbed in a vessel (meaning therefore there is no prohibition on account of Beit Yosef and Beit Yosef — Pri Megadim) is not apparent and common. Raavan and so R' Isserles; and so explicitly in Ran. Magen Avraham note 1. Chak Yosef note 1.",
    "9:_": "(9) There — and it is permitted to retain them, etc. — Pesachim 30a — there is a dispute: Rav said break them and it is forbidden to retain them after Pesach and to use them even not in its kind — a decree lest in its kind; and Shmuel said give them after their time and use them whether in its kind or not in its kind; see there. And the poskim ruled like Shmuel; and even though we are lenient — the halachah is like Rav in prohibitions — this case is different because Shmuel relies on Rashi. Beit Yosef. Bach. Eshel Avraham note 1.",
    "10:_": "(10) There — and one hides them on Pesach, etc. And recently they began placing tin chametz vessels in the winter-house for a gentile, and they say there is no concern lest he use them since they are at height; and likewise Pesach vessels they stand at height all year — this is a complete error; and one must guard them in a place where there is no usual habit lest they err to use them on Pesach. Bach. Shach Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Tur note 1; see there note 1 — and possibly they practiced thus because they are not ben yomo; and even if the master uses them, the majority permit, as written siman 447 seif 10; and all the more so in those whose majority use is in kli sheini or cold, such as flasks — there is no concern; and one who is stringent — a blessing upon him. Magen Avraham note 2. However Chayei Adam note 7 and Eshel Avraham note 1 — to permit simply; see there. But Peri Chadash wrote it is good to be concerned for Bach's reasoning; and so Birkei Yosef note 2; and so Chok Yosef note 2 like Magen Avraham — one who is stringent, a blessing upon him. And so Mekor Chayyim note 5, R' Zalman note 4.",
    "11:_": "(11) However yayin nesech vessels — taste for detriment does not help in them, as Magen Avraham wrote siman 447 note 25; if so, one should forbid; and likewise a gentile's vessel that he has as collateral in a place where they are accustomed to cook in them with vinegar and the like — one should beware, for taste for detriment does not help in them, as explained. Eshel Avraham note 2; and see above siman 442 note 65.",
    "12:_": "(12) And where there is no custom — in any event one may permit in kli sheini; and even in pottery vessels whose use is in kli sheini or via pouring — there is room to permit if one places them in a place where no human hand controls. Responsum at the end of the method of Maharash siman 13; see there b'seder. Mechaber note 1.",
    "13:_": "(13) There. To hide the key — so that one does not go there all the days of Pesach. R' Zalman note 1; and one may also say: because of others who do not know and might open and use them.",
    "14:_": "(14) There. \"For heating, etc.\" — heating that is only from outside does not help to expel absorption; and if you say let one make heating from inside — we are concerned lest it crack (Pesachim 30b). And see below note 21.",
    "15:_": "(15) There — but heating, etc. — hagalah does not help for pottery vessels because the Torah testified regarding pottery vessels that they never leave their mouths. Gemara there. And that heating helps if we are not concerned for them or one returns them to the kiln — as explained below — Tosafot wrote there: this is not considered leaving their mouths, for since they were fired it becomes like a new vessel, for such is the way of pottery vessels when making them — one puts them in a kiln to fire them; see there.",
    "16:_": "(16) There. Rather he poured into them boiling — such as bowls into which one pours from kli rishon. And see below seif 5.",
    "17:_": "(17) There — except that one pours boiling water into them. This is Tur's language; and Beit Yosef explained: even if one did not use pottery vessels boiling, only in kli sheini — heating does not help for them; and he wrote so too Rambam chapter 5 equates pottery vessels used with chametz in kli sheini to using them in kli rishon — until here. However Rabbeinu Moshe z\"l in Darkei Moshe note 1 wrote it does not appear to him Beit Yosef's explanation — rather according to Tur kli sheini has a remedy because it does not absorb; but in the name of Hagahot Maimoniyot he wrote that even if one did not use a pottery vessel except in kli sheini — heating does not help for them; see there; and to this Rabbeinu Moshe z\"l wrote in the gloss on Shulchan Aruch's words that it is Tur's language; and one says: even in kli sheini — meaning that although this language of Tur implies specifically if used via pouring from kli rishon it has no remedy — but if used in kli sheini it has a remedy; one says: even in kli sheini it has no remedy through heating. And so Chayei Adam note 8, Eshel Avraham note 3; and regarding the law see below note 37.",
    "18:_": "(18) There [gloss]. And some forbid even in kli sheini. Meaning: even if he did not pour from the pot into it, only poured from the pot into kli sheini and afterward placed this vessel into it. Magen Avraham note 3. Mekor Chayyim note 6 — but if he poured from bowl to pot, which is pouring of kli sheini — according to everyone it is permitted. Eliyah Rabbah note 5.",
    "19:_": "(19) In the gloss there — and there are those who forbid even in kli sheini. It is proven from here that one holds that even in kli sheini it absorbs something; and so is proven below seif 5; see there note 1 — meaning from what is written below seif 5: and if their use is in kli sheini — kasher them in kli sheini; and if it does not absorb at all — why does it need kashering; rather one must say that in any event it absorbs something — therefore kashering is required.",
    "20:_": "(20) In the gloss there — and there are those who forbid even in kli sheini. And so is Shulchan Aruch's view as written above, or Taz. And so Peri Chadash; and Chayei Adam note 8; and what Magen Avraham note 3 wrote — Shulchan Aruch omitted the law of kli sheini — he retracted from what Beit Yosef wrote; Chok Yaakov challenged him there; and so Eshel Avraham in Hagahot Beit Yosef wrote on Magen Avraham's words mentioned — it requires study; and so HaNe'ehav note 2 wrote to challenge all Magen Avraham's words mentioned; see there. And that which is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 2 — heat of kli sheini b'dieved is permitted — and here one holds heating does not help for it — Eshel Avraham wrote there: perhaps regarding chametz he wanted to be stringent; see there. And regarding the law Chok Yaakov wrote there: in a place of great loss and prevention of Yom Tov joy one may be lenient b'dieved if used in kli sheini if it is not ben yomo — even in pottery vessels; see there. And so Eshel Avraham there. Mekor Chayyim note 6, R' Zalman note 33. Chayei Adam general rule 125 note 3 — meaning such as a spoon made for eating in it whose majority use is in kli sheini — they stick it in a bowl after they poured boiling inside from kli rishon; if they did not kasher and stick on Pesach in kli rishon b'dieved it is permitted if it was not ben yomo and there is great loss and prevention of Yom Tov joy. R' Zalman there. And it is simple this deals if it was clean — for if not, chametz on Pesach forbids in any amount, as is known.",
    "21:_": "(21) There. And even if one fills them with coals, etc. — meaning even if one fills them with coals, through this it becomes like heating from inside that expels absorption — nevertheless it does not help, for we are concerned lest one is worried about them, etc., because their way is not to heat them inside but outside — unlike kuvia and oven in seif 2 where heating inside helps them; since that is their manner we are not concerned and proper heating is done — as explained below.",
    "22:_": "(22) There — lest one be concerned for them, etc. Bach wrote: an oven and stove of pottery whose prohibition comes through heating — even internal heating does not help for them — and so we rule; see there. And so is Peri Chadash's view in seif 2. However Magen Avraham note 4 and Chayei Adam note 9 and Eshel Avraham note 5 challenged him; and they wrote heating from inside helps — for since such is their way we are not concerned lest they crack; see there. And so Beit Meir siman 461; and so Chok Yosef note 4 agreed — not like Bach. And so R' Zalman note 8, Chayei Adam general rule 125 note 2; and see below note 32 and siman 461.",
    "23:_": "(23) There — however if one returns them to the kiln, etc. I was asked: pottery vessels that remained after burning — meaning if it is permitted to use them on Pesach, for it is known this is more than returning to the kiln — and I permitted; and nevertheless everything depends on the matter. Avnei Nezer note 10.",
    "24:_": "(24) There — if one returns them to the kiln, etc. And in Orach Chayyim general rule 58 siman 49 he brought the dispute on this; and wrote therefore we practice not to fire them at all, even in a potter's kiln; see there. And they brought Eshel Avraham note 3; and so Peri Chadash brought the view of those who forbid even to return them to the kiln; and he wrote regarding the law one should be stringent; see there. But Rabbenu Tam in Sefer HaYashar siman 214 wrote they have a remedy through returning to the kiln; and so Mahari Weil Netiv 5 part 2; and in She'eltot of Rav Achai parashat Matot; and so is Tosafot's view (Pesachim 30b); and so Rokeach siman 484; and so R' Menachem chapter 5; see there. And Beit Din Chokat HaPesach note 3; see there. And so R' Zalman note 9; and therefore since there is a dispute — each follows his stream and its plain meaning.",
    "25:_": "(25) There. But for our ovens — no; for there is room to be concerned lest one is worried about them and removes them from the oven before they whiten. Tur in the name of the Rosh — and he means that although in our ovens too they whiten well if left there a long time, nevertheless there is concern lest one not leave them long — unlike a kiln where they whiten even in a short time. Taz note 2. And Maamar Mordechai note 5 wrote the reason: in a kiln, since one heated greatly, as long as they did not crack immediately when placed inside, there is no further concern.",
    "26:א": "(26) There — but for our ovens, no. And see in responsum Be'erot HaMayim part 4 siman 6 who wrote our ovens that are large are permitted; see there. However in derashot of Maharil page 6a he wrote in our ovens it does not help, even the large one of a baker; see there. And so it appears from the stam words of the poskim — one should not distinguish between oven and oven; and in any event even in a kiln some are stringent, as written in the previous note — if so, do not add on it.",
    "26:ב": "(26) And if one placed a pottery vessel in the oven and whitened it until sparks fly and transgressed and cooked in it on Pesach intentionally — forbidden, as one who cooked in a vessel that is not ben yomo intentionally for other prohibitions; and in inadvertence one may say it is permitted. Mishbetzot Zahav note 2; and it appears regarding a poor person and there is Pri Megadim — also for intentional one may be lenient, but one must rebuke him that he transgressed a rabbinic enactment and should not do so again. And see in Sefer Zivchei Tzedek on Yoreh De'ah siman 103 note 23.",
    "27:_": "(27) In the gloss there — forbidden to use it even cold, etc. — meaning in a fixed manner, lest one come to use it hot; and if so, even if they rinsed first it is forbidden; but in an occasional manner it is permitted to use it via rinsing — and not like Orchot Chayim; and a fixed manner — even orally after its prohibition time — it is forbidden to use it. Chayei Adam note 11. Eshel Avraham note 4. Mekor Chayyim note 8. R' Zalman note 2. However with old pottery vessels the custom is not to use them even cold and even in an occasional manner, as written below end seif 22; see there."
  },
  "output/siman_451/levushei-serad/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Chametz all year, etc. — and even if one used chametz during Pesach the law is the same; and so Chok Yaakov.",
    "2:_": "There. However if one returned them, etc. — see Kenesset Yechezkel section 37:",
    "3:_": "There. Forbidden to use it, etc. — see Matteh Yehuda general rule 11 note 6 and siman 17 note 11:",
    "4:_": "Magen Avraham note 4 — it appears in an oven thus; and see Matteh Yehuda general rule 60 note 2:",
    "5:_": "Shulchan Aruch seif 3 — knives they kasher, etc. — see Taz note 17 and Chayei Adam note 90 what he wrote regarding the law if the sheath was not kashered:",
    "6:_": "(Seif 5) They kashered them in a vessel — see Matteh Yehuda general rule 33 note 3:",
    "7:_": "It implies from outside, etc. — it implies that regarding rust too the law is so; and it should read this deals specifically when it formed before its use; and perhaps also in hagalah it does not help — his intent is that from outside one can remove it; and it requires study; examine; and see seif 63; and see Yoreh De'ah note 21.",
    "8:_": "Shulchan Aruch seif 14 — since it drips at all times — see Matteh Yehuda general rule 35 section 21:",
    "9:_": "And in Yoreh De'ah siman 131 seif 1 I wrote it is difficult as it should read.",
    "10:_": "(Gloss seif 23) Even if they are new, etc. — what is cited there as note 29 is a printing error and it is not in its place; rather in the following seif in the gloss \"even if they are new\":",
    "11:_": "(Gloss seif 26) And some are stringent — see Matteh Yehuda general rule 65 note 12:",
    "12:_": "(Taz note 31) Like the first view in siman 447 regarding, etc. — thus it should read:"
  },
  "output/siman_451/rabbi-akiva-eiger/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Seif 1. However if one returned them to the kiln — in Eshel Avraham he wrote in the name of Or Zarua that we do not practice this:",
    "2:_": "It has the law of an earthenware vessel; and if after it dried in the sun it was heated by fire — see Eshel Avraham siman 461.",
    "3:_": "Per what is written in seif 6 in the gloss \"there is not\" — in my humble opinion: behold the foundation of the dispute in seif 6 is whether we follow majority use; and likewise the dispute in seif 4 regarding use through fire whether libun is required — meaning in the Gemara's law regarding knives, the halachah is in boiling water; even though sometimes they use them through fire — meaning since chametz is permitted absorption, even use through fire suffices with hagalah, as is brought in the last chapter of Avodah Zarah regarding notar; if so, there is no need to say we follow majority use; and this is the Mechaber's view in seif 4 and seif 6; and the other views hold chametz is called by its name upon it and is called prohibited absorption, and use through fire requires libun; and the reason for knives is we follow majority use; if so, one of them is proven — either we follow majority use or chametz is called permitted absorption; and in the safek of the law between these two views we hold for chametz that it is called prohibited absorption and also that we do not follow majority use; and this is Rama's ruling in seif 4 and seif 6; and if so, here with knives it is permitted through hagalah.",
    "4:_": "And it is simple — behold we hold in siman 447 seif 4 it is not understood: regarding nullification what of it that its name is upon it and it is called by the name of prohibition — nevertheless we establish it on the law of nullification like all prohibitions; for even if it became mixed orally after midday it is certainly that the name of prohibition is upon it and it is nullified; and since it was nullified and its prohibition ceased, it is entirely permitted; but regarding permitted absorption one may say: since its name is upon it, the name of prohibition is upon it. And likewise regarding nat bar nat — Rashba holds it is not called nat bar nat of permitted absorption of chametz, for its name is upon it. And all this is that it is not called permitted absorption; but it is not more severe than absorption of other prohibitions. Also what Taz wrote \"and furthermore I\" regarding a pan — behold Taz himself in seif 16 wrote there it is through liquid. Also what he wrote \"and it should read this spit deals,\" etc. — still the difficulty from siman 452 is not settled: regarding nat bar nat it is called permitted absorption; if so, even chametz roasted b'eyna suffices with hagalah. And all this requires study.",
    "5:_": "Seif 4 in the gloss — light libun such as digging — requires libun; it appears at first glance that even b'dieved it is forbidden; and in my humble opinion this is specifically if one used it b'eyna; but through a pot such as fish and the like — it is no worse than two pots; see Yoreh De'ah siman 97 and Taz; and so in Mishbetzot Zahav here; and therefore if it became absorbed in a gentile's house one may be lenient:",
    "6:_": "Meaning that if they place straw on it — Taz note 8 and Magen Avraham note 27: if only the hand reaches yad soledes bo from two sides it also helps.",
    "7:_": "And pickled like cooked. At first glance Rama's reason is preferable — for even pouring for three days does not help, for sometimes they use them hot; and if the reason were pouring, pouring three days would help as below seif 21; rather Magen Avraham copied this regarding if on condition we know clearly they were not used hot — nevertheless they are forbidden through rinsing because of dissolving bread crumbs; and in such a case truly pouring three days helps; and nevertheless where sometimes they used hot water — even though it is known they did not use hot chametz — nevertheless through dissolving bread crumbs it absorbs due to pickling; and when they later used hot water in it, the chametz absorbed in them is now absorbed also in this hot water, and pouring does not help.",
    "8:_": "For we do not say absorption travels through the entire vessel. I did not merit to understand — this is when partially hot; but in a vessel in which one cooks it appears sensibly that the hand too is hot and it is impossible to hold it except via a cloth; and the question: if one cooked partially in the vessel — does the entire vessel require hagalah — meaning if it steams above not in the place of the gravy — but in the place of the gravy certainly it steams through the entire thickness of the wall; and if so the hand too is included in the thickness of the wall, unless we say the hand is presumably not one mass until the vessel but is connected with handles and is like two vessels — absorption does not travel from one to the other; if so, it does not depend on the law of Yoreh De'ah siman 121; enlighten my eyes.",
    "9:_": "And as written siman 94. Explanation of Magen Avraham's words: the distinction between that case in siman 92 and that case in siman 94 — in siman 92 we say it absorbed in its entirety; and in siman 94 we say it does not discharge entirely; but in truth it would appear the distinction is only that in siman 92 the pot is entirely hot; but in siman 94 the spoon is not hot; and proof for this — behold it is brought there in siman 94: and one says if the metal spoon is presumed throughout — if part of it is hot, all of it is hot; and this is Rabbeinu Peretz's view — behold if hot throughout, sixty is required against all of it; but in a wooden spoon, and likewise for us in metal, sixty is not required — only against what is inserted in the pot, because the spoon is cold; if so, there is no contradiction here from that case in siman 92; and it is clear.",
    "10:_": "And this is what is written: until the body of the prohibition burns. It was printed in error; and it should read: and if the patch preceded, etc., as written in seif 4 in the gloss; and this is what is written: until all of it burns (Mahari Segal).",
    "11:_": "All this — that one cannot touch it. I question: behold in what one cannot touch it — meaning yad soledes bo — it is not called light libun, since the straw is not burned on it.",
    "12:_": "Like new vessels. See in responsum Maggid MaReishit siman 1 — responsum of Maharit and Maharach Efendi who elaborated on this.",
    "13:_": "That hagalah discharges the entire vessel — this belongs to the seif after it (Mahari Segal).",
    "14:_": "And specifically orally. Meaning then it is permitted even on Pesach — for taste is not returned and revived; but if it became mixed on Pesach it is not nullified; and per my explanation above siman 447 in Magen Avraham note 41 — one should forbid even if mixed before Pesach, since the reason is chametz taste passes by itself into matzah that became forbidden; except matzot are mixed among other matzot — in such a case we say taste is not returned and revived.",
    "15:_": "Nevertheless in barrels it is simple one may be lenient; and at first glance since they are not careful about b'eyna of honey-water, nat bar nat helps automatically — also without hagalah it is permitted; for from the barrels themselves it is three nat and two vessels; see Magen Avraham siman 452.",
    "16:_": "Taz note 27 — and it appears that if one cooked it in water — meaning since Taz decided that for vessels impossible to libun, hagalah helps for yeast and charoset containers; but yayin nesech vessels are stricter because the smell is aroused — therefore it helps to cook first in ashes to remove the smell; but according to what Magen Avraham holds in seif 2 that we practice forbidding yeast containers through hagalah — this repair does not help:",
    "17:_": "Since there is no gravy there. It is wondrous — since there is chametz b'eyna in the place where the pot stands, it is against the gravy and steams inward; and we require sixty; and automatically with chametz it is forbidden in any amount; and if one placed a hot pot on something cold — since the lower overpowers, peel suffices and the pot stands in the place of the peel; but here where everything is boiling — it requires study.",
    "18:_": "There is no yad soledes bo in sweat above. In my humble opinion: behold the cover above is hot and the sweat was heated.",
    "19:_": "Even new ones. And when cooking in them that are not glazed — one should cook them in water before Yom Tov, as is brought below siman 452 in the gloss.",
    "20:_": "Magen Avraham note 49 — not so for chametz. See Manhig general rule 85 note 14:",
    "21:_": "Absorption does not travel through the entire vessel. I did not merit to understand — although absorption does not travel through the entire vessel — meaning the gravy does not steam upward — nevertheless it absorbs through the entire thickness of the vessel; and the absorbed enters the glass lining also; and why did Terumat HaDeshen cite the concern only that they poured boiling inside — automatically they are permitted through hagalah, for the vessel did not absorb except kli sheini; and if so, the absorption did not reach the glass lining which is outside the peel; and even if there is concern the chametz remained inside above and absorbed through pickling — nevertheless the glass is not worse than pottery whose use is cold — hagalah helps, as above seif 21. Also if they cooked in it hot — per Magen Avraham note 45 that all are coated with actual limb — hagalah helps; if so here too let the glass lining outside be like coating with limb; nevertheless a silver vessel is like limb from inside — hagalah helps."
  },
  "output/siman_451/shaarei-teshuvah/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Pots. See Baer Heitev; and he wrote in responsum Chakham Tzvi siman 75 a practical halachic ruling to permit a dish cooked on Pesach in clean chametz pots over which twelve months passed — even for us who forbid nitelaf — nevertheless after twelve months there is no taste at all; and nevertheless his heart was not filled to permit cooking in it l'chatchila; and the Gaon R' Naftali Katz z\"l agreed with him in siman 76; see there siman 80 where he answered the Gaon author of Peri Megadim on this and strengthened his words; see there. And in responsum Chaim Sha'al part 2 siman 38 he wrote Rashba's words in responsum siman 575 are not like Chakham Tzvi who wrote there aging helps only for wine vessels; and he wrote an incident came to his hand in Israel that he was sent a gift of nice porcelain vessels and it was not discernible whether the gentile sender had already used them and two years passed — and they are expensive vessels; and he wrote that at first glance one should not permit l'chatchila as Chakham Tzvi himself wrote; and also from Rashba's words it appears even b'dieved it is forbidden; but here where there is safek-safek perhaps they were not used — and if we say they were used, safek to be lenient — and still requires answer; until here. And it appears to me that if it is possible to kasher them in boiling water three times one should do so; and if they are vessels there is no way to use boiling — yad soledes bo — one may also be lenient; and see Yoreh De'ah siman 122 — these laws.",
    "22:_": "Inside. See Baer Heitev; and see in responsum Maharshadam part 1 siman 21 regarding yufkash (see Peri Chadash — they scatter flour on the griddle and it is only a little and in a moment the dough is baked) — one who guards his soul should distance from it; and Peri Chadash permits; and in Shemesh Tzedakah siman 9 he wrote it is forbidden to throw when they roll the dough to make it thin, to bake it in the oven and cook it afterward; and it is more stringent than what Shulchan Aruch wrote regarding adding flour to the dough; see there the gloss of the Mechaber's son that b'dieved it is permitted.",
    "27:_": "In hagalah. See Baer Heitev; and he wrote in Har HaKarmel siman 5: there is a way when they want to test yayin nesech how much water is in it — they pour from the yayin nesech onto a tin vessel and ignite the yayin nesech to see how much water remains; the tin vessel suffices with hagalah because it becomes immediately flame; and if we say the yayin nesech near the fire and still fire is not yet upon it — it absorbs in the vessel — meaning again it is as absorbed through liquid; see there he wrote proof from a pan in which they fry — Rosh wrote it is not called through fire due to the moisture of the liquid; see there — and there is no proof, for there the fire is outside around the pot, and here the fire is inside and it is absorbed through it; therefore if one used many times in this matter to test yayin nesech — one should be stringent not to permit through hagalah; and nevertheless it appears to permit b'dieved if used through hagalah — for it is close that the nature of yayin nesech is to be after the fire and to be ignited from it; therefore it is not absorbed in the vessel — for immediately when the fire's heat reaches it, it is drawn after the flame and ignites; and all the time one does not feel the fire's heat, there is none who absorbs it; and we find such a case regarding roasting — we say fire draws and absorbs; and even though there is no proof — for there they establish that fire's nature is to draw to itself and not to absorb, which is not so here — who will tell us it is drawn after it, only its heat burns and absorbs also, like milk and similar that ignites and absorbs also; and nevertheless it is somewhat reasonable regarding yayin nesech whose nature is to ignite immediately when fire touches it — fire does not do two actions together; only if the way is to test with a large measure of yayin nesech until it ignites such that the vessel heats from the fire such that yad soledes bo — it requires study lest since the vessel was hot from the fire itself when absorbing from the prohibition not through liquid — hagalah does not help except libun; but it appears in such a case its use is not called through fire — such as a pot or bowl that stood on fire until it was heated greatly yad soledes bo and a prohibited thing was placed in it without liquid — nevertheless its law is not as absorbed through fire literally; and hagalah suffices; for if not, in metal pots and similar if some mishap occurred and the food was forbidden and the pot — they would require libun for the pot even though most times they pour the gravy from the meat and the pot is still boiling yad soledes bo from the fire's heat — and nevertheless they do not require libun for it; if so, since after pouring the gravy they remove it from the fire — what boils by itself from boiling due to the fire does not do the fire's action literally. But one may distinguish as Rosh wrote regarding a pan — one need not require libun even though sometimes the liquid burns until it chars at the bottom of the pot — we have no concern; nevertheless there is moisture of liquid there; see there; and with this one may say all agree regarding a pot from which they pour gravy — nevertheless there is moist moisture; and one may also say: since at the time of the pot's heat through fire there was liquid inside, fire does not control so much to consider it as the fire's heat literally, even though now there is no liquid and it stands in its heat yad soledes bo; and somewhat one may learn this matter — whether products of fire are like fire literally — from Pesachim 74a: an oven they fired and scraped and roasted the Pesach in it — this is not fire-roasting, etc.; see there it depends on two resolutions: if we say the Merciful One revealed fire-roasting twice and we learn from it — if so one may say also here \"every thing that comes in fire\" means the fire's power literally, but products of fire not; and for that resolution that said there if the Merciful One had not revealed we would say the Merciful One was particular only about fire and even if scraped it is fire-roasting; and Rashi explained: the oven's heat is a product of fire and if you put a garment in it, it burns and becomes charcoal and fire; see there — if so here too it depends on two resolutions; and if we say we learn from it — if so likewise \"every thing that comes in fire\" means specifically what was used in fire literally, but what received from products of fire such as an oven that was scraped — not; and at first glance from the Gemara's language there page 76 — it asks and the Pesach was heated from the pottery's heat and the Merciful One said fire-roasting and not roasting due to something else — it implies the first resolution: since the Merciful One revealed fire-roasting twice, automatically its implication is not roasting due to something else; for the second resolution it should have said and the Merciful One said fire-roasting twice — for generally where it is not written twice, even due to something else it is called fire-roasting; and from stam it implies it grasps primarily the first resolution; see there page 74 — they say one does not roast the Pesach on a metal spit because part of it is hot, all of it is hot, and it is heated due to the spit and the Merciful One said fire-roasting and not roasting due to something else, etc.; see there. However from what Tosafot page 75 s.v. ha lo amar etc. wrote — that which needed to answer there to distinguish between wood coals and did not answer the Merciful One revealed in a burn and we learn from it — this is because of the last language above; see there; and if so, since the Gemara discusses according to this last language, it appears somewhat this language is primary; and per this one should study regarding a spit or pan and similar that was heated in fire greatly and is boiling yad soledes bo and forbidden meat or another prohibition b'eyna was placed in it without liquid — whether it requires libun or hagalah, since absorption inside is not through fire literally, only due to products of fire alone; and from Pesachim page 30 — an oven they plastered in its upper story — one does not bake in it until the oven is fired; and for one resolution in metal immediately; and if so it implies even if they plastered in the oven after scraping the fire — nevertheless firing is required and hagalah does not help even though it did not absorb through fire literally, only due to products of fire; and possibly an oven according to its size cannot be inserted into a pot to kasher it — therefore it cites until the oven is fired; and if hagalah were possible, hagalah would suffice; but from the Gemara's language there it implies specifically complete firing; and one may also say: since in any event it did not absorb through liquid — hagalah does not help for it, for the rule \"as it absorbs so it discharges\" does not apply when absorption was not in this manner; therefore it appears libun is required; except light libun suffices and one need not until sparks fly from it; however if at the time the prohibition was absorbed in it the vessel or spit was whitened so much that sparks fly from it — there is no law requiring libun, for this was as absorbed through fire literally and good libun is required until sparks fly from it — so it appears to me. And behold this law is not its place here, only in Yoreh De'ah siman 122; but since incidentally in my girsa it occurred to me here and these matters were renewed to me, I fixed them here; and when I reach their place they will be refined and whitened further, with Heaven's help."
  },
  "output/siman_451/yad-ephraim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Our teacher in Magen Avraham note 1: but absorbed in a vessel is not visible and common — meaning therefore there is no prohibition due to lo yeira'eh velo yimatzei.",
    "2:_": "In Taz note 1: perhaps he will forget once and bring vessels and use, etc. — as it should read. And with this they did not decree due to lo pelug. It requires study — for due to this reason he should also permit placing chametz vessels on Pesach in a high place, as Magen Avraham note 2 wrote; and from Taz's words it appears here he agrees to Bach that it is forbidden; and in my humble opinion in any event it is not applicable to say lo pelug except at the time of the prohibition itself — such as placing chametz vessels on Pesach in a low place, where certainly it is forbidden lest one use them; and therefore also in a high place it is forbidden due to lo pelug; and it is literally comparable to the case of a candle on Shabbat; not so if one places Pesach vessels all year — it is simple lo pelug is not applicable — for what prohibition is in the matter, even low, since there is no chametz prohibition yet; only because he wants it to be permitted to use them on Pesach therefore he places them where the hand does not control — lo pelug of Rabbanan is not applicable here, since no prohibition applies here at all; only the main concern is on Pesach itself, as it is said: just as it is forbidden to use vessels standing in a low place lest one forget and use them — likewise it should be forbidden to use even vessels standing in a high place due to lo pelug; it is simple this is not comparable to the candle case — for here even in vessels standing in a low place themselves, if it were clear one would never use them with chametz — it is permitted to use them on Pesach; and therefore those standing in a high place there is no reason to forbid due to lo pelug; and if you come to forbid them because although standing at height nevertheless perhaps he forgot once and used chametz — for this we are not concerned, since it is nitelaf and is only Pesach stringency; even in low automatically in height one may permit; and this is Taz's intent regarding \"perhaps he will forget,\" etc. — one need not be concerned, for it is only stringency and due to lo pelug they did not decree here, for this is not comparable to the candle case for the reason I wrote; and even if this is not Taz's intent — so it appears to me the matters are correct in their reasoning; and furthermore so it appears to me another reason to distinguish: regarding Pesach vessels standing at height one must be concerned for two forgettings — lest he forget and use vessels with chametz, and afterward forget and return them to their high place above; and it is simple we are not concerned for this; and just as we are not concerned simply lest he forget and take chametz vessels and place them at height among Pesach vessels — all the more so here where we would need to be concerned he lowers them and after using them with chametz returns them among the Pesach vessels standing there; and a reasonable matter — for all this we are not concerned; not so with chametz vessels standing on Pesach — properly there is concern lest he forget and lower them and use them all the days of Pesach.",
    "3:_": "Magen Avraham note 3 — this is kli sheini; and so Rif — meaning also Rif wrote like Rambam's language and uses kli sheini, such as bowls, etc., upon which they pour boiling, etc.; and meaning also like their use via pouring; and what Magen Avraham wrote \"and in all cases\" — also wrote such as bowls — meaning Rambam wrote in all cases also such as bowls deals; and presumably similar to bowls written regarding metal vessels — meaning they were used via pouring.",
    "4:_": "Note 4 — heating does not help for it to be considered like a new vessel; but, etc. — it need not be like a new vessel — immediately, etc.; however in Maharil he wrote it is not called like kilns — meaning and if so internal heating also does not help except Exceptionally when fired two or three days — for the main elevation in returning to the kiln is because the flame rises to them from every side and they do not crack; but nevertheless it does not discharge kiln heat more than other internal heating; therefore if we say a kiln requires heating two or three days regarding discharging the prohibition from within — if so, even if one wants to kasher a pottery oven where lo pelug applies lest it crack, one needs also that it be fired two or three days to discharge the prohibition from within through internal heating; however at first glance it is possible per Rosh's words and so Shulchan Aruch here — the reason in returning to kilns: since they insert it for such great heating, certainly we are not concerned; if so one may say Maharil holds specifically with great heating of two or three days lo pelug is not applicable; but regarding discharge Maharil concedes stam heating suffices; and if so in a pottery oven where lo pelug is not applicable, stam internal heating suffices even if not fired two or three days; but per the language written here in Shulchan Aruch — since they insert for such great heating, certainly we are not concerned — if so one may say Maharil wrote specifically for this that it is not called like kilns except when fired two or three days; if the heating is not so great one may say properly we are concerned; but in a pottery oven where in any event lo pelug is not applicable — one may say Maharil concedes whenever heated from inside to the measure that it whitens, this suffices.",
    "5:_": "In Taz note 8 — meaning Mordechai not so regarding spits, etc. — it appears he meant that Mordechai there deals with a pan in which they bake pashitida — the pashitida is not meat or milk b'eyna, but there is also meat or milk inside; and since it is not absorbed from b'eyna literally through fire — properly this reason of permitted absorption suffices with this libun alone; but if what was absorbed is from b'eyna literally through fire — such as an oven in which they baked without liquid, or a spit on which they roasted chametz b'eyna — this reason of permitted absorption alone does not help to permit through hagalah; and by law complete libun is required; and it is close that there is a printing error and Taz's words need emendation; and it should read: except regarding vessels in which they bake things absorbed from permitted, etc.; and nevertheless his words require study — for behold Mordechai's proof is from Avodah Zarah who distinguishes between permitted absorption — and there it deals with spits and skewers on which kodshim meat was roasted b'eyna and became notar — and even so he says one kashered them because permitted was absorbed.",
    "6:_": "In Magen Avraham note 13 — such as bowls, Beit Yosef, etc. — this is Rabbenu Tam, Beit Yosef — meaning Beit Yosef brought in the name of Rif and Rosh regarding their use in kli sheini, such as bowls, etc.; and what Magen Avraham wrote \"and not bowls literally,\" etc. — meaning Shulchan Aruch here that wrote their use in kli sheini does not mean bowls literally as is the explanation in Rif and Rambam's words; rather here he means bowls are called kli sheini — and if one inserts a spoon, etc.",
    "7:_": "There note 37 — but if one inserts the side that was not kashered it is forbidden, etc. — his words are wondrous in my humble opinion — for he wrote: and if the spoon was inserted, etc. — this is from the words of Hagahot Orach Chayyim at the end of the siman; and there it is explained not like his words — for behold he wrote one must kasher the spoon l'chatchila another time and insert it entirely into the pot — is it that other times one kashered and it is not like deception — for the food is permitted and the spoon requires hagalah immediately, since it was cooked in a pot that is not ben yomo — until here; and if according to Magen Avraham's words — why need a reason due to deception other times; and also what concern of deception applies here — for behold one must kasher it again and insert it entirely into the pot so as not to come to insert the other side that was not kashered and come to prohibition; rather certainly this is what he said: there is no distinction in the matter; and even inserting in the side that was not kashered is kosher, since that side was not inserted in prohibition — as Hagahot Orach Chayyim wrote there: the reason is because as it absorbs so it discharges."
  },
  "output/siman_451/chokhmat-shlomo/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Seif 1 — pottery pots used for chametz all year etc. In my humble opinion see Taz s.k. 2 — his words contradict the Mechaber's reason for returning to kilns; and see what I wrote on this in my work on Yoreh De'ah laws of kashering vessels siman 121, on Yoreh De'ah edition 6 siman 23; see there and examine. See Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot s.k. 2 who wrote that if one transgressed and whitened a pottery vessel and cooked in it, b'dieved the dish is permitted in inadvertence; in intentional sin he left undecided. See my work on Orach Chayim year 515 daf 83a in responsum to the community of Batishan — I wrote the opposite that in every case it is forbidden; see his reason and examine.",
    "2:_": "(There seif 8 in the gloss) and likewise bone vessels require hagalah. Behold the source of this law is from Mordechai brought in Beit Yosef, and his language: it is written in the name of Or Zarua — it appears to me that bone vessels do not require hagalah because they do not absorb; and so explained R' Yitzchak of Corbeil that we find no absorption that forbids regarding bone; and I practice as I wrote — but my heart strikes me somewhat, for we say chapter Kelei Midian \"and every goat product\" etc. — Rashi explained to bring horns, hooves, and bones; all the more a bone vessel and an ox-horn vessel — apparently there is absorption in bone vessels, since it says \"you shall purify yourselves\"; end of his words. See what I wrote on this in my work earlier — wonder at him. Now I was asked by someone: what proof from there — behold there it deals with tumah, and for kashering of Midian it says afterward through Elazar; and this is truly wondrous. To resolve this it would appear thus: one may say a vessel that does not absorb is not a vessel even for tumah; proof — we see glass vessels do not receive tumah from the Torah, only Rabbanan decreed tumah on them — necessarily because it does not absorb it is not considered a vessel for tumah; if so here too if bone does not absorb it is not considered a vessel even for tumah. Further proof from what we learn Shabbat chapter 1 daf 16b — there it asks: metal vessels are d'oraisa, as written \"only the gold and silver\"; and it is difficult to reverse — behold the verse in kashering of Midian deals, and from where for tumah; and if from \"that which cannot go through fire you shall pass through water\" — behold there it deals with immersion from a gentile from law of immersion of 48, not from tumah; and from where that metal vessels receive tumah from the Torah — necessarily to reverse from absorption. Proven it is a vessel for tumah and the same for reverse — certainly one and the same; examine. It would appear more through pilpul according to what we learn in Zevachim daf 96 — it asks: make a Temple pottery oven that they heat from inside; and answers: we do not make service vessels of pottery. Difficulty: if bone does not absorb, make of wood — necessarily bone also absorbs; but perhaps wood is not a vessel at all and we do not make service vessels from it — therefore he brought proof from what is said in Midian \"and every goat product you shall purify\" — it is a vessel in any event; and since it is a vessel in any event, difficulty — make an oven of wood; necessarily wood also absorbs; and it comes out well as above temporarily. What he does not bring proof from the mishnah that bone vessels are tamei — one may say it is only d'rabbanan like glass — therefore he had to bring proof from scripture etc. I found afterward in Tosafot Chullin daf 21 they wrote thus; see there and examine well."
  }
};
